[170.747469, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:11]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m Topic for t8HK3GC0u: Tabs or spaces? (Ctrl-C to stop)\r\n"]
[170.758637, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:10]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou·human\u001b[0m> [human] You are Alice. Make the case for spaces and discuss it with Bob in this thread. Wait for his ready message before Alice 1. Post\r\n                            at most 45 words per turn, labeled Alice 1, Alice 2, Alice 3. Each later turn must answer a specific Bob objection before raising\r\n                            your next concern. Wait for Bob between turns; no polling. After Bob joins, use --require-ack-pane bob for each post. Only after\r\n                            Bob 3, post Shared recommendation with your conclusion.\r\n"]
[170.761061, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:10]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m alice·claude joined\r\n"]
[170.762796, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:10]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou·human\u001b[0m> [human] You are Bob. Make the case for tabs and discuss it with Alice in this thread. After joining, tell Alice you are ready, then wait\r\n                            for Alice 1. Post at most 45 words per turn, labeled Bob 1, Bob 2, Bob 3. Each turn must answer a specific Alice objection before\r\n                            raising your next concern. Wait for Alice between turns; no polling. Use --require-ack-pane alice for each post. Only after Alice\r\n                            posts Shared recommendation, confirm it or state a remaining tradeoff.\r\n"]
[170.762824, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:11]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33myou·human\u001b[0m> [human] Please settle on one recommendation and explain the tradeoff, after three replies each.\r\n"]
[170.765196, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:11]\u001b[0m \u001b[1;34m-!-\u001b[0m bob·codex joined\r\n"]
[177.923028, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:11]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob·codex\u001b[0m> Alice, I am ready to make the case for tabs. Please send Alice 1.\r\n"]
[210.180885, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:11]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice·claude\u001b[0m> Alice 1: Spaces render identically everywhere: editors, diffs, GitHub, terminals, code review tools. With tabs, alignment of\r\n                       continuation lines and comments breaks whenever someone uses a different tab width. Spaces make the file look exactly as the author\r\n                       wrote it.\r\n"]
[230.547844, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:12]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob·codex\u001b[0m> Bob 1: Different tab widths break column alignment, agreed. Tabs for indentation and spaces for alignment preserve structure while\r\n                    allowing readers their preferred indentation width. A formatter can enforce that distinction. Why should the author decide every reader’s\r\n                    indentation width, especially for accessibility?\r\n"]
[241.248668, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:12]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice·claude\u001b[0m> Alice 2: Accessibility is fair, but tabs-indent/spaces-align is fragile: mixed whitespace is invisible, and most formatters (Prettier,\r\n                       Black, gofmt aside) default to spaces. New concern: line-length limits. With tabs, an 80-column rule is undefined, since width varies\r\n                       per reader.\r\n"]
[257.628322, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:12]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob·codex\u001b[0m> Bob 2: Mixed whitespace needs formatter enforcement; unsupported tooling weakens my proposal. An 80-column check can use a documented tab\r\n                    width while readers choose another display width. That sacrifices universal visual limits. Could enforcing spaces unnecessarily exclude\r\n                    readers who need wider or narrower indentation?\r\n"]
[272.329965, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:12]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice·claude\u001b[0m> Alice 3: Exclusion risk is real but mitigable: editors can render leading spaces at a custom width (e.g. VS Code/Vim indent-guides,\r\n                       elastic display). Next concern: copy-paste into web forms, Markdown, chat and YAML, where tabs get mangled or are outright invalid.\r\n"]
[288.711479, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:13]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob·codex\u001b[0m> Bob 3: YAML forbids indentation tabs, and paste targets can damage them; spaces travel better. Indent guides alone do not change space\r\n                    width, so accessibility still deserves explicit tooling. My remaining concern is imposing one convention where a language formatter\r\n                    already mandates tabs.\r\n"]
[303.426451, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:13]\u001b[0m <\u001b[33malice·claude\u001b[0m> Shared recommendation: Default to spaces, enforced by formatter and .editorconfig, except where a language formatter mandates tabs\r\n                       (gofmt, Makefiles): follow it. Tradeoff: spaces give portability and consistent line limits but lose per-reader indent width; I\r\n                       concede indent guides do not fix that.\r\n"]
[319.799072, "o", "\u001b[2m[08:13]\u001b[0m <\u001b[31mbob·codex\u001b[0m> Confirmed: default to spaces with formatter and .editorconfig enforcement; honor language tooling and required tab syntax, including\r\n                    Makefile recipes. The tradeoff is portability and consistent visual limits versus adjustable indentation width. Teams should address\r\n                    accessibility explicitly when choosing editor support.\r\n"]
[352.650412, "i", "\u0003"]
[352.650627, "o", "^C"]
[352.659269, "o", "\u001b[1;36myou $ \u001b[0m"]
